WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.142
The first published NIST PQC standards are not the end of the migration story.

00:00:05.142 --> 00:00:09.104
Additional algorithms remain important because cryptographic migration is a long program,

00:00:09.104 --> 00:00:10.905
not a single product upgrade.

00:00:10.905 --> 00:00:14.507
Falcon was selected as an additional digital signature algorithm path,

00:00:14.507 --> 00:00:18.469
and NIST has continued work toward a separate standard for it.

00:00:18.469 --> 00:00:23.151
The standardized naming and final implementation expectations should be tracked as guidance matures.

00:00:23.151 --> 00:00:26.032
HQC was selected as a backup key-establishment algorithm.

00:00:26.032 --> 00:00:30.354
Backup choices matter because organizations should not rely on a single design

00:00:30.354 --> 00:00:33.236
family forever when the ecosystem is still maturing.

00:00:33.236 --> 00:00:37.558
A backup KEM can provide another option if future analysis, implementation experience,

00:00:37.558 --> 00:00:42.240
or operational requirements create a need for diversity beyond the primary key-establishment standard.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.842
Primary choices, backup choices, and future standards play different roles.

00:00:45.842 --> 00:00:50.884
A primary standard is the main reference point for near-term implementation and vendor support.

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:54.126
A backup choice helps manage longer-term risk and diversity.

00:00:54.126 --> 00:00:57.727
A future standard may become relevant as products, validation programs,

00:00:57.727 --> 00:00:59.168
and protocol support develop.

00:00:59.168 --> 00:01:01.689
Treating every status as identical creates confusion.

00:01:01.689 --> 00:01:07.452
Migration programs should track these algorithms without chasing every draft as if it were already production-ready.

00:01:07.452 --> 00:01:12.495
The right posture is informed readiness: know what is coming, ask vendors about roadmaps,

00:01:12.495 --> 00:01:14.656
design systems that can change algorithms,

00:01:14.656 --> 00:01:18.617
and avoid hard-coding assumptions that will make the next transition expensive.

00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:24.020
PQC readiness is partly about adopting standards and partly about building the ability to adapt.
